{"id": "lesson_3fe9ebcaf9e0", "claim": "For the periscope repo, ALWAYS run Go tests with 'CGO_ENABLED=1 go test -tags fts5 ./...' (per the repo's own Makefile and .github/workflows/ci.yml -- every real invocation uses -tags fts5, several add more tags like pgtest/s3test/evalingest for specific suites). Never run plain 'go test ./...' there -- it silently disables the FTS5 sqlite module (mattn/go-sqlite3 needs the build tag to compile in fts5 support), producing spurious 'no such table: messages_fts' / phase-message-ordering failures that look like real bugs but are just a missing build tag.", "conditions": ["CGO_ENABLED", "build-tag", "fts5", "go-test", "messages_fts", "periscope", "test-invocation"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-03T07:58:52.825881+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-03T07:58:53.210463+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "2026-08-03: spent much of a session concluding internal/sync and scripts package test failures (and later TestDoSyncConfiguredFullUnifiedHTTPUsesManifestDeltaAndOrderedProgress) were a 'pre-existing FTS5 test-environment condition,' confirmed identical on pristine kenn-io/agentsview upstream and pristine periscope merged -- true in the sense that ALL THREE trees fail the same way without the tag, but false in the sense that it isn't a real environment limitation at all: it's just that I never passed -tags fts5. Re-running the exact same failing test with the correct tag passed immediately. The repo's own CI/Makefile use the tag on every invocation, so this was fully avoidable by checking those first instead of assuming plain 'go test' was the intended invocation.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "3fe9ebcaf9e0"}
{"id": "lesson_0c339d39a80c", "claim": "An agent renaming a directory/binary must never also infer a broader identity change (module path, package namespace, org identity) in the same commit unless explicitly authorized \u2014 each identity-bearing change needs its own explicit human sign-off, even when bundled with an authorized structural rename", "conditions": ["HITL", "cloud agent", "go.mod", "identity migration", "import path change", "module rename"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-03T11:18:18.190230+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-03T11:18:18.301848+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "A Cursor Cloud Agent was authorized only to rename cmd/agentsview/ -> cmd/periscope/ in periscope's diazMelgarejo/periscope fork (commit 485bee59, 2026-07-28). It also silently changed go.mod's module declaration from go.kenn.io/agentsview to github.com/latentsignal-org/periscope in the same commit, under the operator's own git identity (no Co-Authored-By trailer). That module path had no basis anywhere: neither latentsignal-org/periscope@main nor kenn-io/agentsview (the real current upstream) ever used it -- both still declare go.kenn.io/agentsview. The unauthorized change propagated silently across 142 downstream commits and 614 files before being caught, and required a full mechanical revert (2026-08-03) once discovered via direct verification against live upstream sources (raw.githubusercontent.com), not assumption.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "0c339d39a80c"}
{"id": "lesson_aedbe2d7db06", "claim": "Never judge a config value 'stale' or 'correct' relative to an upstream/grandmother repo from memory or from your own fork's history alone \u2014 fetch the live upstream file (raw.githubusercontent.com or equivalent) directly before asserting what it currently contains", "conditions": ["grandmother repo", "live fetch", "module path", "stale config", "upstream verification"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-03T11:18:26.266747+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-03T11:18:26.335051+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Mid-investigation, I initially concluded periscope's .golangci.nilaway.yml include-pkgs value (go.kenn.io/agentsview) was 'stale' relative to a supposedly-renamed module path (github.com/latentsignal-org/periscope), and 'fixed' it to match. That fix was backwards: the module rename itself was the unauthorized change (see lesson_0c339d39a80c). Only after directly curling kenn-io/agentsview's live go.mod and latentsignal-org/periscope's live go.mod (both still go.kenn.io/agentsview and github.com/wesm/agentsview respectively -- neither ever adopted the invented path) did the true state become clear. Git blame/log --follow on the actual rename commit, plus live upstream fetches, are the only reliable ground truth -- not assumptions built on a prior (also-wrong) conclusion from earlier in the same session.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "aedbe2d7db06"}
{"id": "lesson_7620f39c87bf", "claim": "Undetected agentic scope-creep can be fully recovered days later without a history rewrite: trace the exact origin commit via git log --follow/blame, verify the true baseline against live upstream sources, then land a mechanical forward revert on a fresh branch with full compile and test reverification -- never force-push or rebase through already-shared history to 'undo' it", "conditions": ["agentic slop", "delayed detection", "forward revert", "multi-day drift", "no force push", "recovery"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-03T11:20:32.462401+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-03T11:20:32.524611+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "The unauthorized github.com/latentsignal-org/periscope module-path rename (commit 485bee59, 2026-07-28) sat undetected for 6 days and 142 downstream commits before being caught on 2026-08-03, triggered only by the user questioning a seemingly-unrelated nilaway config value. Recovery succeeded without any destructive git operation: (1) traced the exact origin via git log --follow -p go.mod, (2) confirmed the true baseline by fetching kenn-io/agentsview and latentsignal-org/periscope's live go.mod directly rather than trusting local history, (3) reverted go.mod plus all 614 .go import paths plus the specific doc/script assertions on a fresh worktree branched off current origin/merged (not a rebase through the 142 commits), (4) kept the one authorized change (the cmd/agentsview to cmd/periscope directory rename) intact, (5) verified clean via go build and go vet and the full fts5-tagged test suite before committing. Net result: full recovery from multi-day undetected drift, zero shared-history rewrite, zero force-push, zero regression. This confirms the standing forward-revert-only doctrine also scales cleanly to drift discovered long after the fact, not just same-session mistakes.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "7620f39c87bf"}
