{"id": "lesson_49e8701ba9e9", "claim": "Activating a provider config in a tool's own settings store (e.g. Cline's providers.json) does not make the CLI use it at runtime -- check for a separate top-level provider-selection flag/default before assuming a config write took effect", "conditions": ["cline", "provider config", "runtime default", "silent misroute", "verify config takes effect"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-05T04:53:06.424628+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-05T04:53:06.524640+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Wrote a corrected wandb-deepseek-v4-flash profile into Cline's providers.json openai-compatible slot, updated lastUsedProvider, then ran a bare 'cline' invocation expecting it to route there. It silently used a completely different provider (openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.2) instead -- lastUsedProvider is bookkeeping, not a runtime default. Cline's real top-level -P/--provider flag defaults to its own hosted 'cline' provider regardless of what's configured elsewhere. Even passing -P openai-compatible alone wasn't enough: it then used a stale cached model from a previously-active profile. Both -P and -m had to be passed explicitly on every invocation before the write actually took effect end-to-end. Verified via -v's run_start line reporting the actual providerId/modelId used, not by trusting the config file alone.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "49e8701ba9e9"}
{"id": "lesson_3c85823ef46f", "claim": "A vendor-doc's exact sample curl/Python for a third-party OpenAI-compatible API is not guaranteed correct -- an optional-looking parameter (an OpenAI-Project header, or the SDK's project= kwarg) can silently break auth (401) even though the underlying key is valid; test with and without each optional param before trusting the documented sample verbatim", "conditions": ["auth 401", "openai-compatible", "optional header breaks auth", "vendor sample code", "wandb"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-05T04:53:18.475609+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-05T04:53:18.567156+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "wanDB.ai's own reference sample (curl + Python openai SDK) included an OpenAI-Project header / project= kwarg alongside the API key. Running it exactly as documented produced 401 invalid_api_key. Confirmed independently via three paths -- raw curl, the official openai Python SDK, and a separate /v1/models GET with no project param at all -- that the key itself is valid and omitting the project header/kwarg entirely returns real completions. The project field is very likely for Weave/W&B tracing scope, not request auth, but including it in the auth-bearing request breaks auth outright on this account. Never assume a vendor's own quickstart sample is bug-free; isolate each header/param independently when a documented example fails.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "3c85823ef46f"}
{"id": "lesson_0c78b132c55b", "claim": "When extracting or inspecting a secret from a config file via a shell one-liner, never use a bare grep/cat on the raw file -- pipe through a redaction step or use a script that only reports length/presence, even when the extraction command's PRIMARY purpose looks safe (e.g. checking a var name exists)", "conditions": ["grep secrets", "redaction bug", "secret leak", "transcript exposure"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-05T04:53:31.560843+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-05T04:53:31.664207+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Leaked a live secret into a conversation transcript twice in one session: once via a Python redaction helper that only checked top-level dict keys for 'key'/'token'/'secret' and missed a nested apiKey field, and again via a plain 'grep -n' on a .env.local file that printed full KEY=value lines including two real credentials. Both times the intent was innocuous (checking config shape / whether a var was set), but the mechanical command chosen printed the raw value anyway. The fix going forward: always use length-only checks (${#VAR} chars) or a script that explicitly strips values before printing, never a bare grep/cat/json.dumps on a file known or suspected to contain secrets -- regardless of how confident the redaction logic looks, because shallow (non-recursive) checks miss nested fields and grep has no concept of redaction at all.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "0c78b132c55b"}
{"id": "lesson_a10088cab4ae", "claim": "GNU coreutils flock(1) (the standalone command) is not present on macOS/BSD by default -- confirmed missing even on a fully-set-up dev machine, not just a fresh install. Never gate a bash script's mutual-exclusion on the flock command; use Python's fcntl.flock() instead (a real POSIX syscall wrapper available in Python's stdlib on Darwin) if the script already shells out to python3, or the portable mkdir-is-atomic lock idiom if it's pure bash.", "conditions": ["bash locking", "concurrency", "flock", "macos", "portability"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-05T06:49:30.048576+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-05T06:49:30.180753+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Wrote 'exec 9>lockfile; flock 9' in a new bash+python provider-swap script, following the pattern I assumed was portable. Confirmed via 'command -v flock' that it's simply absent on this macOS machine -- would have failed for every user of the script, not just an edge case. This exact gotcha was already documented elsewhere in this same codebase (exa-mcp-wrapper.sh's own comments) but I didn't check for it before writing new locking code, so I nearly reintroduced a bug the codebase had already solved once.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "a10088cab4ae"}
{"id": "lesson_7ef854a99c2d", "claim": "Second-precision timestamps used as unique filenames (e.g. for rotating backup/version history) will silently collide under any workload faster than 1 call per second -- including two successive calls from the same automated script or an agent iterating quickly. Always use microsecond (or finer) precision, a monotonic counter, or a uuid suffix for filename-based versioning, never bare %Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ.", "conditions": ["backup rotation", "filename uniqueness", "rapid succession", "test caught bug", "timestamp collision"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-05T06:49:40.082405+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-05T06:49:40.184970+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "A rotating backup-history feature (10-slot cap, one snapshot per config transition) used strftime('%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ') for backup filenames. A pytest test that switched between 3 profiles in rapid succession caught the second-precision collision immediately: the second and third backup writes silently os.replace'd over the first, losing history entirely, discovered only because the test asserted exact backup contents rather than just count. Fixed by switching to '%Y%m%dT%H%M%S.%fZ'. This validates writing a real behavioral test (not just manual spot-checks) even for logic that already 'worked' in a slower, hand-run trial -- the bug only appeared under realistic rapid-fire conditions.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "7ef854a99c2d"}
{"id": "lesson_d232d1a1d91d", "claim": "A plan/audit document's own 'Done' status table is not proof the work is actually done for every row -- verify each claimed-complete item against the real filesystem/content individually, the same rigor as verifying an external upstream claim, just scoped internally to this repo's own docs.", "conditions": ["done status table", "internal audit", "plan doc verification", "stale claim"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-05T06:49:50.911647+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-05T06:49:51.004452+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "orama's 2026-08-03-hermes-openclaw-graft-audit-plan.md Wave 0 table marked 8 items 'Done' (taxonomy doc, REGISTRY.yml dispatch_lane field, SKILL.md lane tags, a $OPENCLAW_ROOT scrub across references/results/*.md, etc). Checked all 8 individually against the actual filesystem/grep content rather than trusting the table: 7 were genuinely true, but the $OPENCLAW_ROOT scrub claim was false for one specific file (fleet-2026-07-27-rtx3080-hermes-brain-merge.md still had two literal path references). A 7/8 pass rate on an internal 'Done' table is exactly the kind of partial-truth a status table can hide -- each row needs independent verification, not a spot check of a couple.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "d232d1a1d91d"}
{"id": "lesson_322a2fba2337", "claim": "For any tool that hot-swaps between named configs carrying live credentials (API keys, provider profiles), never write directly into the live config file inline -- always run through: (1) a checked-in *.tmpl with ${VAR} placeholders holding zero real secrets, (2) a resolver that fills placeholders from a canonical secrets/<name> (0600) + .env.<name> loader pair, (3) an idempotent snapshot of the outgoing state into a rotating, capped backup history stored OUTSIDE the git repo (since a snapshot holds a real resolved secret), before the atomic write. This is now the established convention on this machine for every provider secret set up this session (Firecrawl, GitHub Copilot, Cline's Google/wanDB/BigModel profiles) -- follow it for any new one rather than inventing a new scheme.", "conditions": ["credential storage", "provider config", "secrets management", "session backup", "uniform pattern"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-05T06:50:03.282073+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-05T06:50:03.464614+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Built this pattern incrementally across one long session while parametrizing Firecrawl/GitHub-Copilot/Cline secrets that had been sitting as plaintext in ~/.claude.json and Cline's own providers.json. Also discovered orama-system's glm52-fallback/setup-glm52.sh had already independently arrived at the same secrets/<name>+.env.<name> shape -- confirms convergent validity, not just my own preference. Worth capturing centrally so the NEXT secret (any provider, any tool) gets set up consistently on the first attempt instead of drifting into a fourth slightly-different scheme.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "322a2fba2337"}
{"id": "lesson_11baf3e69fcb", "claim": "When a concurrent agent commits directly to the same working directory/branch mid-review, verify by reading actual diffs and running the actual test suite before crediting or trusting any of its claimed fixes -- never trust a commit message's claimed test count.", "conditions": ["commit message", "concurrent agent", "git log", "verify before crediting"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-05T20:10:59.849236+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-05T20:10:59.987021+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Discovered live during a /autoplan Eng-phase review on orama-system's hermes-openclaw-graft-audit-plan.md (2026-08-06): a 'Relay Cursor Agent' committed 5 commits directly to the exact branch/working-directory in use, implementing fixes matching the review's own in-progress task IDs (T-ENG-1 etc). Verified by reading diffs + running pytest (38/38 passed, matching the claimed count) before crediting any of it as done -- this is the productive counterpart to the 'concurrent agent collisions' risk already in memory, but the discipline (verify, don't trust) is identical either way.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "11baf3e69fcb"}
{"id": "lesson_fb76280fc2cd", "claim": "Independent dual-voice AI review (Codex + a separate Claude subagent, no shared context) that converges on the identical bug by reading the same code independently is the strongest verification signal available -- weight it above either voice's severity rating alone.", "conditions": ["autoplan", "codex", "cross-model", "dual voice", "independent review"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-05T20:11:00.389060+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-05T20:11:00.474261+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "In the same /autoplan session, both an internal Claude subagent and an external Codex CLI call independently found the exact same two bugs (hermes-delegate's as_completed()/timeout misuse causing a silent-forever deadlock, and hermes_spawn.sh's cleared-before-echo empty-PID bug) with zero shared context between them, across 2 separate review phases. A third independent voice (DX-phase subagent) found the same deadlock bug a third time. Cross-model replication, not stated severity, is the strongest confidence signal a multi-voice review can produce.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "fb76280fc2cd"}
{"id": "lesson_2c4124092607", "claim": "The EXA daemon-wrapper pattern (singleton daemon + Unix-socket bridge to avoid N duplicate npx child processes) only applies to locally-spawned stdio MCP servers -- it does not transfer to type:http MCP servers like Firecrawl/GitHub Copilot, since Claude Code connects to those directly via HTTP with no local backend process to daemonize.", "conditions": ["EXA pattern", "MCP consolidation", "daemon wrapper", "http transport"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-05T20:11:00.864787+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-05T20:11:00.953429+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Learned during MCP consolidation work this session (OpenClaw/.mcp.json -> global ~/.claude.json): initially assumed the EXA pattern should be emulated for Firecrawl+GitHub per the user's explicit request, but on inspection Firecrawl and GitHub Copilot are both type:http in the config, meaning the 'N duplicate local processes' problem the EXA pattern solves doesn't exist for them. The applicable part of EXA's pattern that DOES transfer is the secret-resolution architecture (bearer_token_env_var -> ~/.openclaw/secrets/<name> 0600 -> .env.<name> loader), which was already implemented separately.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "2c4124092607"}
{"id": "lesson_d7f233fdd47e", "claim": "An agent renaming a directory/binary must never also infer a broader identity change (module path, package namespace, org identity) in the same commit unless explicitly authorized \u2014 each identity-bearing change needs its own explicit human sign-off, even when bundled with an authorized structural rename. When citing live go.mod values after such an incident, verify each repository independently: GitHub repo lineage (wesm/agentsview redirects to kenn-io/agentsview) does not imply a fork already adopted the parent vanity module path.", "conditions": ["HITL", "cloud agent", "fork lag", "go.mod", "identity migration", "import path change", "live go.mod", "module rename", "vanity module path"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-03T15:12:43.479133+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-03T15:13:00.793363+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Correction for lesson_0c339d39a80c (PR #321 / CodeRabbit + owner clarification 2026-08-03). Live re-verify at remediation time: kenn-io/agentsview declares module go.kenn.io/agentsview; latentsignal-org/periscope@main still declares module github.com/wesm/agentsview; github.com/wesm/agentsview HTTP-redirects to kenn-io/agentsview (same repo lineage after the wesm\u2192kenn-io rename). Upstream vanity migration is commit 6ef281c74db5 (\"Use go.kenn.io vanity module path\"); the latentsignal fork never received that go.mod change. The original 0c339d39a80c notes incorrectly stated that latentsignal-org/periscope@main also declares go.kenn.io/agentsview \u2014 aedbe2d7db06 was correct that the fork still has github.com/wesm/agentsview. HITL claim stands; only the live-path evidence needed this append-only supersession. Unauthorized invented path github.com/latentsignal-org/periscope remains absent from both live trees.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": "lesson_0c339d39a80c", "source_candidate": "d7f233fdd47e"}
{"id": "lesson_b1cfecbc4eb6", "claim": "Meta-instruction, in effect until the v2 oramasys org multi-repo centralization is complete: before pushing a fix to either orama-system or Perpetua-Tools, check whether the same file, or the same underlying concept, exists in the sibling repo and whether it's actually in sync -- not just \"did I apply the fix I meant to apply,\" but \"is the BEST version of this fix now present in both repos, chosen by comparing them directly, not by assuming whichever repo I fixed first is automatically correct.\" Where the two repos' versions differ, the better one (more defensive pattern, more complete investigation notes, more accurate glob/scope) should be adopted in both, not just the one being actively worked on. This bidirectional check is a stopgap for the current two-repo-with-manual-sync architecture; once v2's centralized package/multi-repo structure lands, this class of drift should become structurally impossible rather than something that needs checking by hand every time.", "conditions": ["accurate", "actively", "actually", "adopted", "apply", "architecture", "assuming", "automatically", "become", "before", "best", "better", "bidirectional", "both", "centralization", "centralized", "check", "checking", "chosen", "class", "comparing", "complete", "concept", "correct", "current", "defensive", "differ", "directly", "drift", "effect", "either", "every", "exists", "file", "first", "fix", "fixed", "glob", "hand", "impossible", "investigation", "just", "lands", "meant", "meta-instruction", "more", "multi-repo", "needs", "notes", "now", "once", "one", "orama-system", "oramasys", "org", "package", "pattern", "perpetua-tools", "present", "pushing", "rather", "repo", "repos", "same", "scope", "sibling", "something", "stopgap", "structurally", "structure", "sync", "than", "them", "time", "two", "two-repo-with-manual-sync", "underlying", "until", "version", "versions", "whether", "whichever", "worked"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-04T21:11:24.526502+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-04T21:11:30.609889+00:00", "reviewer": "host-agent", "rationale": "Explicit meta-instruction from the user, scoped to remain in effect until v2 oramasys org multi-repo centralization removes the need for manual bidirectional sync-checking.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "b1cfecbc4eb6"}
